# ─── Line endings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One rule for the whole repo: every text file is LF, in the object database
# AND in the working tree, on every platform. Not "whatever the checkout did".
#
# This is not tidiness. The suite is full of SOURCE-SCAN tests — checks that
# read a component's own text and assert on it, because a scoped `<style>`
# block and a template's structure are invisible to a jsdom mount. Several of
# them slice on an anchor like `indexOf('.reward-stage\n')`. In a CRLF file
# that anchor never matches, `indexOf` returns -1, `slice(-1)` yields the last
# character, and the assertion runs against "\n" — so the failure reads as
# "this component lost a rule it must never lose" when the truth is that a
# tool wrote the file with Windows endings. That cost real debugging time
# twice in one session, from two different directions.
#
# Mixed endings had already crept in from several tools, so `eol=lf` is set
# explicitly rather than left to `core.autocrlf`: the working tree is where
# the tests read, so the working tree is what has to be LF.
#
# Applying it to files that already exist takes one command, which is
# deliberately NOT run for you:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#
# It stages every file it touches, which is a decision for whoever is holding
# the branch — not a side effect of adding this file.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ─── Binary: never normalised, never diffed as text ──────────────────────────
#
# `text=auto` guesses, and a guess that goes wrong on a binary corrupts it —
# a webp with an LF-looking byte pair is not a file you want "fixed". These
# say so outright.

*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.ogg   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.wav   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.mov   binary
*.webm  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip   binary
*.pdf   binary

# ─── Generated ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Still LF (it is text, and the rule above holds), just collapsed in diffs and
# kept out of language statistics.

pnpm-lock.yaml  linguist-generated=true  -diff
